  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+1)^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(2t+1)^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{65}{8}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{65}{8} & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {[2(1)+1]}^{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{65}{8} & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (81)+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{65}{8}-\\frac{81}{8} \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{8}(2t+1)^{4}-2.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(2t+1)^{4}-2 & =0 \\\\ (2t+1)^{4} & =16 \\\\ 2t+1 & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-\\frac{3}{2}\\,,\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSince $t>0$, we discard solution $t=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ \nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$",
